    "description": "A reporting platform must run recurring jobs through a reusable execution engine. The engine owns the complete lifecycle: it discovers enabled job definitions, prepares a tenant-specific context, starts a transaction, invokes the job, records the result, and decides whether to retry after a failure. Each customer supplies an operation that receives the prepared context and returns a report result; it must not call the engine's transaction, retry, or recording steps itself. The platform supports jobs written by several business teams, and a deployment may enable different jobs for different tenants. Job implementations must remain unaware of the engine internals, while the engine must be able to add tracing, timeout enforcement, and retry behavior without modifying every job. Registration is required because the engine discovers job definitions from configured modules, but the central decision being evaluated is who owns the execution sequence and when application behavior is invoked.",
    "decision_statement": "Choose whether the reporting engine or each customer job controls the run lifecycle, including setup, invocation, result recording, and retries.",
    "notes": "inversion-of-control fits the engine-owned run sequence invoking customer jobs at extension points; registry is the near miss for the job-discovery axis, with dependency-injection supporting engine assembly."
  },
